Course Objective
The objective of this course is to understand the concepts of Duck Creek Billing and its underlying core functionalities. At the end of this module, you would gain a broader functional understanding of each of the below concepts:
•	Introduction to Duck Creek Billing
•	Bill plans & Scheduling
•	Invoicing & Payment Processing
•	Payment Allocation
•	Follow Up Process
•	Suspense & Disbursement
•	Commission
•	Chart of Accounts Mapping
1) Introduction to Duck Creek Billing
The billing component of Duck Creek is used by insurers to create bills, collect money, and manage all billing-related activities for policies.
Think of it like: The central cashier and accounting system for an insurance company.
2) Bill Plans & Scheduling
The rules that decide how much to bill and when (e.g., monthly, quarterly, annually).
Think of it like: Choosing a mobile plan (prepaid/postpaid) and the billing cycle.
3) Invoicing & Payment Processing
Invoicing: Creating and sending the bill that shows amount due, due date, and breakdown of charges.
Payment Processing: Accepting money via different channels (online, bank transfer, card, cash, etc.), validating it, and recording it against the right invoice/policy.
4) Payment Allocation
Deciding where a received payment should be applied, e.g., to the latest bill, oldest bill, fees, or specific policy.
Think of it like: Splitting a deposit across multiple outstanding bills.
5) Follow Up Process
Activities are done when payments are late or missing.
Think of it like: Automated reminders + human collections steps.
6) Suspense & Disbursement
Suspense (Holding Account): Temporary place to park money when you can’t immediately match it to the right invoice/policy (e.g., missing details).
Disbursement: Moving money out of suspense to the correct destination once identified—or refunding to the customer/agent.
7) Commission
What it is: Money paid to agents/brokers for selling policies—often a percentage of premium.
How billing relates: Commissions can be calculated, accrued, and paid based on collected premiums, with rules for claw backs/adjustments if a policy cancels or changes.
8) Chart of Accounts Mapping
What it is: Connecting billing transactions (premium, fees, taxes, commissions, refunds) to the correct accounting ledger codes.
Think of it like: Labeling every transaction so the finance system knows exactly where it goes.

Module 1: Introduction to Duck Creek Billing, Bill Plan and Scheduling
Introduction to Duck Creek Billing
1) Duck Creek Billing Overview
•	What it is: A billing system for insurance companies that handles all billing-related tasks.
•	Why it matters: It connects Policy, Billing, and Claims into one platform for smooth operations.
________________________________________
2) Integrated Suite
•	Duck Creek combines Policy, Billing, and Claims so insurers don’t need separate systems.
•	Think of it like: A single app that manages everything—policy details, bills, and claims.
________________________________________
3) Single Solution Across LOB & Bill Types
•	LOB = Line of Business (e.g., Auto, Home, Commercial).
•	Duck Creek Billing works for all types of insurance and billing methods.
•	Benefit: No need for multiple billing systems for different products.
________________________________________
4) Holistic View
•	Shows all transactions and payment history in one place.
•	Why useful: Easy to track customer payments and resolve issues quickly.
________________________________________
5) Account Driven
•	All billing activities happen under a billing account.
•	Example: If a customer has multiple policies, they can be linked to one billing account for easier management.
________________________________________
6) Policy Linked to Billing Account
•	Every insurance policy is tied to a billing account.
•	Think of it like: Your Netflix subscription linked to your payment account.
________________________________________
7) Direct Bill & Agency Bill
•	Direct Bill: Customer pays insurer directly.
•	Agency Bill: Customer pays agent, and agent pays insurer.
•	Why important: Supports different business models.
________________________________________
8) Timer and Event Driven
•	Timer: Automated tasks run at scheduled times (e.g., generate bills every month).
•	Event: Actions happen when something triggers them (e.g., payment received → update account).
•	Think of it like: 
o	Timer = Alarm clock for scheduled tasks.
o	Event = Doorbell that triggers an action when pressed.

Bill Plan & Scheduling
1) Bill Plan
•	What it is: A plan that decides how and when the customer will pay the premium.
•	Why important : It gives flexibility to customers and helps insurers manage cash flow.
________________________________________
2) Two Key Parameters
•	Collection Method: How the payment will be collected. 
o	Paper: Customer gets an invoice and pays manually.
o	Recurring Card: Payment is auto charged to a credit card.
o	Auto Pay Bank: Payment is auto deducted from a bank account.
•	Payment Plan: How many installments and how often payments happen. 
o	Example: Monthly, quarterly, or annual payments.
o	Includes down payment and installment distribution.
________________________________________
3) Scheduling
•	What it is: The timeline for generating bills and collecting payments.
•	Think of it like: A calendar that decides when invoices are sent and payments are due.
•	Example: 
o	If 12 installments → schedule monthly billing dates.
 

